 ('b-day12', 3, 'وقيل لثنتي عشرة خلت منه نص عليه ابن إسحاق', 'نص عليه ابن إسحاق', 'reported',
  'It was also said: on the twelfth of it. Ibn Ishaq stated this.',
  'One of several views on the day of the month. Ibn Kathir goes on to call this the view best known among the majority, “and Allah knows best”.'),
 ('b-year', 3, 'قال ابن إسحاق: وكان مولده عليه الصلاة والسلام عام الفيل', 'وبعث على رأس أربعين سنة من الفيل.', 'established',
  'Ibn Ishaq said: His birth, blessings and peace be upon him, was in the Year of the Elephant, and this is what is well known from the majority. Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami said: It is something none of our scholars doubts, that he, blessings and peace be upon him, was born in the Year of the Elephant, and was sent as a prophet at the head of forty years from the Elephant.',
  ''),
 ('b-qays', 3, 'وقال محمد بن إسحاق حدثني المطلب بن عبد الله بن قيس بن مخرمة', 'كنا لدين.', 'established',
  'Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: al-Muttalib ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhrama told me, from his father, from his grandfather Qays ibn Makhrama, who said: “I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were born in the Year of the Elephant. We were the same age.”',
  ''),
 ('b-other', 3, 'وقال آخرون بل كان عام الفيل قبل مولد رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بعشر سنين.', 'بعشر سنين.', 'reported',
  'Others said: the Year of the Elephant was ten years before the birth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.',
  'Ibn Kathir lists this and other views (twenty-three, thirty and forty years) and calls some of them very strange.'),
 ('b-agreed', 3, 'قال خليفة بن خياط والمجتمع عليه', 'ولد عام الفيل.', 'established',
  'Khalifa ibn Khayyat said: What is agreed upon is that he, peace be upon him, was born in the Year of the Elephant.',
  ''),
 ('b-amina', 3, 'فذهب كما تقدم فزوجه أشرف عقيلة في قريش', 'بن زهرة الزهرية', 'established',
  '[ʿAbd al-Muttalib] went, as mentioned before, and married him [his son ʿAbdullah] to the noblest lady of Quraysh: Amina bint Wahb ibn ʿAbd Manaf ibn Zuhra, of the clan of Zuhra.',
  'The words in brackets are added to show who is meant.'),
 ('b-father', 3, 'والمقصود أن أمه حين حملت به توفي أبوه', 'على المشهور.', 'established',
  'The point is that when his mother was expecting him, his father ʿAbdullah died while he was still unborn, according to the well-known view.',
  ''),
 ('b-madinah', 3, 'خرج عبد الله بن عبد المطلب إلى الشام إلى غزة', 'ورسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يومئذ حمل.', 'established',
  'ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib went out to al-Sham, to Gaza, with one of the caravans of Quraysh carrying goods to trade. They finished their trading and turned back, and passed by Madinah. ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib was ill that day, and said: “I will stay behind with my uncles’ family, Banu ʿAdi ibn al-Najjar.” He stayed with them, ill, for a month, and his companions went on and reached Makkah. ʿAbd al-Muttalib asked them about his son ʿAbdullah, and they said: “We left him with his uncles’ family, Banu ʿAdi ibn al-Najjar, and he is ill.” ʿAbd al-Muttalib sent his eldest son al-Harith to him. He found that he had died and been buried in the house of al-Nabigha, and he went back to his father and told him. ʿAbd al-Muttalib, and ʿAbdullah’s brothers and sisters, grieved for him deeply. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was then still unborn.',
  'From al-Waqidi, through Muhammad ibn Saʿd. Told gently in the game: nothing of the death is shown.'),
 ('b-28', 3, 'قالا: توفي عبد الله بن عبد المطلب بعد ما أتى', 'وقيل سبعة أشهر.', 'reported',
  'They both said: ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib died after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had lived twenty-eight months. And it was said: seven months.',
  'Ibn Saʿd, quoted next by Ibn Kathir, says the first view (that he died before the birth) is firmer.'),
 ('b-orphan', 3, 'والذي رجحه الواقدي وكاتبه', 'وأعلى مراتبه.', 'established',
  'What al-Waqidi and his scribe, the hafiz Muhammad ibn Saʿd, judged most likely is that his father died while he, blessings and peace be upon him, was still unborn in his mother’s womb. This is the deepest kind of being an orphan, and its highest rank.',
  'Ibn Kathir’s own words.'),
 ('b-kharbudh', 3, 'قال: توفي عبد الله بالمدينة ورسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ابن شهرين', 'فأوصى به إلى عمه أبي طالب.', 'reported',
  'He said: ʿAbdullah died in Madinah when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was two months old. His mother died when he was four years old, and his grandfather died when he was eight years old, and entrusted him to his uncle Abu Talib.',
  'Narrated by al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Ibn Kharrabudh. It differs from the firmer reports about his father and his mother, so it is shown only as a report.'),
 ('b-halima', 3, 'قال محمد بن إسحاق: فاسترضع له عليه الصلاة والسلام من حليمة بنت أبي ذؤيب', 'من حليمة بنت أبي ذؤيب', 'established',
  'Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: A nurse was found for him, blessings and peace be upon him: Halima bint Abi Dhuʾayb.',
  'Halima was from the tribe of Saʿd ibn Bakr, of Hawazin, as the book goes on to say.'),
 ('b-milk', 3, 'وإخوته عليه الصلاة والسلام - يعني من الرضاعة -', 'وهي الشيماء', 'established',
  'And his brother and sisters, blessings and peace be upon him, by nursing: ʿAbdullah ibn al-Harith, Unaysa bint al-Harith, and Hudhafa bint al-Harith, who is al-Shaymaʾ.',
  ''),
 ('b-grand', 3, 'فكان رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم مع أمه آمنة بنت وهب، وجده عبد المطلب', 'لما يريد به من كرامته', 'established',
  'So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with his mother Amina bint Wahb and his grandfather ʿAbd al-Muttalib, in Allah’s care and keeping. Allah made him grow up well, for the honour He intended for him.',
  'Ibn Ishaq, after the years with Halima.'),
 ('b-abwa', 3, 'أن أم رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم آمنة توفيت وهو ابن ست سنين', 'فماتت وهي راجعة به إلى مكة', 'established',
  '… that the mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Amina, died when he was six years old, at al-Abwaʾ, between Makkah and Madinah. She had brought him to visit his uncles’ family, Banu ʿAdi ibn al-Najjar, and she died on the way back with him to Makkah.',
  'Ibn Ishaq, from ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hazm. Told gently in the game: nothing of the death is shown.'),
 ('b-abbas', 3, 'فلما هلك عبد المطلب بن هاشم ولي السقاية وزمزم بعده ابنه العباس', 'وأقرها في يده رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم.', 'established',
  'When ʿAbd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim died, the watering of the pilgrims and Zamzam passed after him to his son al-ʿAbbas, one of the youngest of his brothers. It stayed with him until Islam came, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left it in his hands.',
  ''),
 ('b-abutalib', 3, 'وكان رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بعد جده عبد المطلب مع عمه أبي طالب', 'وكان إليه ومعه.', 'established',
  'After his grandfather ʿAbd al-Muttalib, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with his uncle Abu Talib, because ʿAbd al-Muttalib had entrusted him to him, and because Abu Talib was the full brother of his father ʿAbdullah: their mother was Fatima bint ʿAmr ibn ʿAʾidh ibn ʿImran ibn Makhzum. Abu Talib was the one who took charge of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after his grandfather; he was in his care and with him.',
  ''),

 # ── Episode 4 · The Trustworthy One ──
 ('t-shepherd', 4, 'ما بعث الله نبيا إلا راعي غنم', 'بالقراريط "', 'established',
  '[The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:] “Allah did not send any prophet except that he was a shepherd.” His companions said to him: “And you, O Messenger of Allah?” He said: “And I tended them for the people of Makkah for a few qirats.”',
  'A hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari, from Abu Hurayra. A qirat is a small coin.'),
 ('t-cause', 4, 'وكان سببه أن', 'فأبوا أن يعينوا على العاص بن وائل', 'established',
  'Its cause was that a man from Zubayd came to Makkah with goods. Al-ʿAs ibn Waʾil bought them from him and then held back what he owed him. The man from Zubayd asked the allied clans, ʿAbd al-Dar, Makhzum, Jumah, Sahm and ʿAdi ibn Kaʿb, to help him against him, and they refused to help against al-ʿAs ibn Waʾil.',
  'About Hilf al-Fudul.'),
 ('t-call', 4, 'فلما رأى الزبيدي الشر أوفى على أبي قبيس', 'فنادى بأعلى صوته', 'established',
  'When the man from Zubayd saw how badly he was treated, he climbed up on Abu Qubays at sunrise, while Quraysh were sitting in their gatherings around the Kaʿbah, and called out at the top of his voice.',
  'Abu Qubays is the mountain beside the Kaʿbah. The book then gives his lines of poetry.'),
 ('t-pact', 4, 'فقام في ذلك الزبير بن عبد المطلب', 'فدفعوها إليه.', 'established',
  'Al-Zubayr ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib stood up about it and said: “This cannot be left.” So Hashim, Zuhra and Taym ibn Murra gathered in the house of ʿAbdullah ibn Judʿan. He made food for them, and they made an alliance in Dhu al-Qaʿda, a sacred month. They bound themselves and promised by Allah to be one hand with the wronged against the wrongdoer until his right was returned to him, as long as the sea wets a tuft of wool and as long as Thabir and Hiraʾ stand in their places, and to help one another in their living. Quraysh called that alliance Hilf al-Fudul, and said: “These people have taken on something of great merit.” Then they walked to al-ʿAs ibn Waʾil, took the man from Zubayd’s goods from him, and gave them back to him.',
  'Hilf al-Fudul means “the alliance of merit”.'),
 ('t-fudul', 4, 'لقد شهدت في دار عبد الله بن جدعان حلفا لو دعيت به', 'وألا يعد ظالم مظلوما', 'established',
  '[The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:] “I witnessed, in the house of ʿAbdullah ibn Judʿan, an alliance which, if I were called to it in Islam, I would answer. They allied to return what is owed to its rightful people, and that no wrongdoer would overpower someone wronged.”',
  'Narrated by al-Humaydi from Sufyan ibn ʿUyayna.'),
 ('t-trade', 4, 'قال ابن إسحاق: وكانت خديجة بنت خويلد امرأة تاجرة', 'وخرج معه غلامها ميسرة حتى نزل الشام', 'established',
  'Ibn Ishaq said: Khadija bint Khuwaylid was a merchant woman of standing and wealth. She would hire men to trade with her money for a share of the profit. When she heard about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, about the truthfulness of his speech, the greatness of his trustworthiness and the nobility of his character, she sent for him and offered him to go out to al-Sham trading with her money, and she would give him more than she gave the other traders, with a young man of hers called Maysara. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted it from her and went out with her goods, and her young man Maysara went with him, until he reached al-Sham.',
  ''),
 ('t-profit', 4, 'فلما قدم مكة على خديجة بمالها باعت ما جاء به، فأضعف أو قريبا', 'فأضعف أو قريبا', 'established',
  'When he came back to Makkah to Khadija with her goods, she sold what he had brought, and it came to double, or nearly.',
  ''),
 ('t-qualities', 4, 'يا بن عم إني قد رغبت فيك', 'وصدق حديثك', 'reported',
  '“Son of my uncle, I wish to marry you because of our kinship, your standing among your people, your trustworthiness, your good character and the truthfulness of your speech.”',
  'Khadija’s words, which Ibn Ishaq gives with “fima yazʿumun”, “as they claim”. So it is shown as a report.'),
 ('t-marry', 4, 'قال ابن هشام: وكان عمر رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم حين تزوج خديجة خمسا وعشرين سنة', 'منهم أبو عمرو المدني', 'established',
  'Ibn Hisham said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was twenty-five years old when he married Khadija. More than one of the people of knowledge told me so, among them Abu ʿAmr al-Madani.',
  ''),
 ('k-dates', 4, 'وقال ابن إسحاق: كان الفجار بعد الفيل بعشرين سنة.', 'والمبعث بعد بنائها بخمس سنين.', 'established',
  'Ibn Ishaq said: al-Fijar was twenty years after the Elephant. The building of the Kaʿbah was fifteen years after al-Fijar, and his being sent as a prophet was five years after its building.',
  'Al-Fijar was a war between tribes before Islam. Other reports give other spans; this one agrees with Ibn Ishaq’s age of thirty-five at the building.'),
 ('k-stones', 4, 'لما بنيت الكعبة ذهب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ينقل الحجارة', 'ينقل الحجارة', 'established',
  'When the Kaʿbah was built, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to carry stones.',
  'From the hadith of Jabir in al-Bukhari and Muslim; only its opening is used here.'),
 ('k-age', 4, 'فلما بلغ رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم خمسا وثلاثين سنة اجتمعت قريش لبناء الكعبة', 'فأرادوا رفعها وتسقيفها', 'established',
  'When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was thirty-five years old, Quraysh gathered to build the Kaʿbah. They had been wanting to do it, to give it a roof, but they were afraid to take it down. It was only loose stones laid without mortar, a little higher than a man, and they wanted to raise it and roof it.',
  'Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('k-ship', 4, 'وكان البحر قد رمى بسفينة إلى جدة لرجل من تجار الروم.', 'فأخذوا خشبها فأعدوه لتسقيفها.', 'established',
  'The sea had thrown up a ship at Jeddah, belonging to a merchant of the Romans, and it was broken up. They took its timber and made it ready for the roof.',
  ''),
 ('k-carpenter', 4, 'وكان بمكة رجل قبطي نجار', 'بعض ما يصلحها.', 'established',
  'There was in Makkah a Coptic man who was a carpenter, and he got ready for them some of what was needed.',
  ''),
 ('k-pure', 4, 'فقال: يا معشر قريش لا تدخلوا في بنيانها من كسبكم إلا طيبا.', 'إلا طيبا.', 'established',
  'He said: “People of Quraysh, put nothing of your earnings into its building except what is good and lawful.”',
  'Said by Abu Wahb ibn ʿAmr, the uncle of the Prophet’s father ﷺ, according to Ibn Ishaq. The book goes on to list kinds of unlawful earnings.'),
 ('k-sides', 4, 'ثم إن قريشا تجزأت الكعبة.', 'وهو الحطيم .', 'established',
  'Then Quraysh divided the Kaʿbah between them. The side of the door was for Banu ʿAbd Manaf and Zuhra. Between the Black Corner and the Yemeni Corner was for Banu Makhzum and the clans of Quraysh who joined them. The back of the Kaʿbah was for Banu Jumah and Sahm. The side of al-Hijr was for Banu ʿAbd al-Dar ibn Qusayy, Banu Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzza and Banu ʿAdi ibn Kaʿb: that is al-Hatim.',
  ''),
 ('k-walid', 4, 'ثم إن الناس هابوا هدمها وفرقوا منه.', 'أساس إبراهيم عليه السلام', 'established',
  'Then the people were afraid to take it down, and held back from it. Al-Walid ibn al-Mughira said: “I will begin taking it down for you.” He took the pickaxe and stood on it, saying: “O Allah, no harm is meant. O Allah, we want nothing but good.” Then he took down part of it, at the two corners. The people waited that night and said: “We will see. If something happens to him, we will not take any of it down, and we will put it back as it was. If nothing happens to him, then Allah is pleased with what we have done in taking it down.” In the morning al-Walid went back to his work, and took it down, and the people took it down with him, until they reached the foundation, the foundation of Ibrahim, peace be upon him.',
  'In the game the House is never taken down or touched: the building is studied on a separate wooden model.'),
 ('k-gather', 4, 'قال ابن إسحاق: ثم إن القبائل من قريش جمعت الحجارة لبنائها', 'دون الأخرى.', 'established',
  'Ibn Ishaq said: Then the clans of Quraysh gathered stones to build it, each clan gathering on its own. Then they built it until the building reached the place of the Corner [the Black Stone], and they quarrelled over it: every clan wanted to raise it to its place, and not the others.',
  ''),
 ('k-nights', 4, 'فمكثت قريش على ذلك أربع ليال أو خمسا', 'فتشاوروا وتناصفوا.', 'established',
  'Quraysh stayed like that for four or five nights. Then they gathered in the Mosque, consulted one another and agreed to be fair.',
  'Before this, the book says the clans were close to fighting over it.'),
 ('k-first', 4, 'فزعم بعض أهل الرواية أن أبا أمية بن المغيرة', 'ففعلوا.', 'reported',
  'Some of the narrators claim that Abu Umayya ibn al-Mughira ibn ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn Makhzum, who was then the oldest man in all of Quraysh, said: “People of Quraysh, let the first person who comes in through the gate of this Mosque judge between you in what you disagree about.” And they did so.',
  'Ibn Kathir introduces the name of the speaker with “zaʿama”, “some claim”.'),
 ('k-amin', 4, 'فكان أول داخل دخل', 'وكانت قريش تسمي رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم الأمين.', 'established',
  'The first one to come in was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When they saw him, they said: “This is al-Amin, the trustworthy one. We are content. This is Muhammad.” When he reached them and they told him about it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Bring me a cloth.” It was brought, and he took the Corner [the Black Stone] and placed it in it with his own hand. Then he said: “Let each clan take hold of a side of the cloth, then lift it all together.” They did so, until when they had brought it to its place, he set it there with his own hand ﷺ. Then the building went on over it. Quraysh used to call the Messenger of Allah ﷺ al-Amin.',
  ''),

 # ── Episode 5 · The First Revelation (vol. 3, the chapter on how the revelation began) ──
 ('r-age', 5, 'وذكر أول شئ أنزل عليه من القرآن العظيم', 'أربعون سنة.', 'established',
  '… and the first thing of the Mighty Qurʾan to be sent down to him. That was when he ﷺ was forty years old.',
  'The chapter heading and its first line.'),
 ('r-43', 5, 'وحكى ابن جرير عن ابن عباس وسعيد بن المسيب', 'ثلاثا وأربعين سنة.', 'reported',
  'Ibn Jarir reported from Ibn ʿAbbas and Saʿid ibn al-Musayyib that he was forty-three years old at that time.',
  'Given by Ibn Kathir as another view (“hakā”, he reported).'),
 ('r-place', 5, 'وهو جبل بأعلى مكة على ثلاثة أميال منها', 'والغار في تلك الحنية', 'established',
  '[Hiraʾ] is a mountain in upper Makkah, three miles from it, on the left of whoever goes to Mina. It has a peak that leans over, facing the Kaʿbah, and the cave is in that bend.',
  'Ibn Kathir’s own description.'),
 ('r-jiwar', 5, 'كان رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يجاور في حراء في كل سنة شهرا', 'ثم يرجع إلى بيته', 'established',
  'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to stay at Hiraʾ for one month every year, in devotion. That was something Quraysh held dear in the time before Islam. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stay that month every year, feeding the poor who came to him. When he had finished his month, the first thing he did when he left, before going into his house, was go to the Kaʿbah and go round it seven times, or as many as Allah willed. Then he would go back to his house.',
  'From ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, through Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('r-dreams', 5, 'أول ما بدئ به رسول الله صلى الله عليه وآله من الوحي', 'مثل فلق الصبح', 'established',
  '[ʿAʾisha said:] The first of the revelation that came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was true dreams in his sleep. He did not see a dream but it came true like the breaking of dawn.',
  'From the hadith of ʿAʾisha in Sahih al-Bukhari.'),
 ('r-hira', 5, 'ثم حبب إليه الخلاء', 'حتى جاءه الحق وهو في غار حراء.', 'established',
  'Then being alone was made dear to him. He would go alone to the cave of Hiraʾ and worship there for a number of nights, before going back to his family, and he would take provisions for that. Then he would go back to Khadija and take provisions for the same again, until the truth came to him while he was in the cave of Hiraʾ.',
  'Sahih al-Bukhari, continued.'),
 ('r-iqra', 5, 'فجاءه الملك', 'ثم أرسلني فقال:', 'established',
  'The angel came to him and said: “Read.” He said: “I am not one who reads.” He said: “Then he took hold of me and pressed me until I was exhausted, then let me go and said: ‘Read.’ I said: ‘I am not one who reads.’ He took hold of me and pressed me a second time until I was exhausted, then let me go and said: ‘Read.’ I said: ‘I am not one who reads.’ He took hold of me and pressed me a third time until I was exhausted. Then he let me go and said:”',
  'Sahih al-Bukhari, continued. The verses that follow, the opening of Surah al-ʿAlaq, are recited by the reciter in the game, not narrated. Nothing of this is shown.'),
 ('r-qari', 5, 'فالصحيح أن قوله', 'لست ممن يحسن القراءة.', 'established',
  'The correct view is that his words “I am not one who reads” are a denial: that is, “I am not someone who knows how to read.”',
  'Ibn Kathir’s explanation.'),
 ('r-return', 5, 'فرجع بها رسول الله صلى الله عليه وآله يرجف فؤاده', 'لقد خشيت على نفسي.', 'established',
  'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went back with them, his heart trembling, and went in to Khadija bint Khuwaylid. He said: “Cover me, cover me.” They covered him until the fear left him. He told Khadija what had happened and said to her: “I feared for myself.”',
  'Sahih al-Bukhari, continued.'),
 ('r-kalla', 5, 'فقالت خديجة: كلا والله لا يخزيك الله أبدا.', 'وتعين على نوائب الحق', 'established',
  'Khadija said: “No, by Allah, Allah will never disgrace you. You keep the ties of kinship, you honour the guest, you carry the burden of the weak, you give to those who have nothing, and you help in the hardships that come by right.”',
  'Sahih al-Bukhari, continued.'),
 ('r-waraqa', 5, 'فانطلقت به خديجة حتى أتت ورقة بن نوفل', 'أنصرك نصرا مؤزرا.', 'established',
  'Then Khadija took him to Waraqa ibn Nawfal ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzza, Khadija’s cousin. He had become a Christian in the time before Islam. He used to write in Hebrew, and wrote from the Injil in Hebrew as much as Allah willed. He was a very old man who had become blind. Khadija said to him: “Cousin, listen to your nephew.” Waraqa said: “Nephew, what do you see?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him what he had seen. Waraqa said: “This is the Namus who came down to Musa. I wish I were young now. I wish I could be alive when your people drive you out.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Will they drive me out?” He said: “Yes. No man has ever brought what you have brought without being treated as an enemy. If I live to see your day, I will support you with all my strength.”',
  'Sahih al-Bukhari, continued. The Namus means the angel who brought revelation.'),
 ('r-pause', 5, 'ثم لم ينشب ورقة أن توفي وفتر الوحي فترة.', 'وفتر الوحي فترة.', 'established',
  'Then before long Waraqa died, and the revelation paused for a while.',
  'The lines after this, which al-Zuhri gives as “what reached us”, are not used.'),
 ('r-muddathir', 5, 'بينا أنا أمشي إذ سمعت صوتا من السماء', 'فأنزل الله:', 'established',
  '[He ﷺ said:] “While I was walking, I heard a voice from the sky. I raised my eyes, and there was the angel who had come to me at Hiraʾ, sitting on a chair between the sky and the earth. I was afraid of him. I went back and said: ‘Cover me, cover me.’ Then Allah sent down:”',
  'From Jabir ibn ʿAbdullah, in Sahih al-Bukhari. The verses that follow, the opening of Surah al-Muddaththir, are recited by the reciter in the game.'),
 ('r-monday', 5, 'وهكذا قال عبيد بن عمير وأبو جعفر الباقر', 'وهذا ما لا خلاف فيه بينهم.', 'established',
  'So said ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, Abu Jaʿfar al-Baqir and more than one of the scholars: that revelation came to him on a Monday. There is no disagreement among them about this.',
  ''),
 ('r-rabi', 5, 'ثم قيل: كان ذلك في شهر ربيع الأول', 'في شهر ربيع الأول', 'reported',
  'Then it was said: that was in the month of Rabiʿ al-Awwal.',
  'One of the views Ibn Kathir lists (“qila”, it was said).'),
 ('r-ramadan', 5, 'والمشهور أنه بعث عليه الصلاة والسلام في شهر رمضان', 'ومحمد بن إسحاق وغيرهما.', 'established',
  'The well-known view is that he was sent as a prophet in the month of Ramadan, as ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, Muhammad ibn Ishaq and others stated.',
  ''),
 ('r-17', 5, 'وروى الواقدي بسنده عن أبي جعفر الباقر', 'وقيل في الرابع والعشرين منه.', 'reported',
  'Al-Waqidi narrated with his chain from Abu Jaʿfar al-Baqir: the revelation to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began on a Monday, the seventeenth night of Ramadan. And it was said: on the twenty-fourth of it.',
  'Two views on the night within Ramadan.'),

 # ── Episode 6 · The First Believers ──
 ('f-secret', 6, 'وجعل رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يذكر جميع ما أنعم الله به عليه', 'سرا إلى من يطمئن إليه من أهله.', 'established',
  'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began to speak of all that Allah had blessed him and His servants with, of prophethood, in secret, to those of his family he trusted.',
  'Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('f-khadija', 6, 'وقال ابن إسحاق: وكانت خديجة أول من آمن بالله ورسوله', 'وصدق بما جاء به.', 'established',
  'Ibn Ishaq said: Khadija was the first to believe in Allah and His Messenger, and to accept as true what he brought.',
  ''),
 ('f-ali', 6, 'ثم إن علي بن أبي طالب - رضي الله عنه - جاء بعد ذلك بيوم وهما يصليان.', 'ففعل علي وأسلم', 'established',
  'Then ʿAli ibn Abi Talib came a day after that, while the two of them were praying. ʿAli said: “Muhammad, what is this?” He said: “The religion of Allah, which He chose for Himself and sent His messengers with. I call you to Allah alone, who has no partner, and to His worship, and to reject al-Lat and al-ʿUzza.” ʿAli said: “This is something I have never heard of before today. I will not decide anything until I have spoken to Abu Talib.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not want his secret spread before his call was made public, so he said: “ʿAli, if you do not accept Islam, then keep it to yourself.” ʿAli stayed that night, and then Allah put Islam into ʿAli’s heart. In the morning he came early to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “What did you offer me, Muhammad?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “That you bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, who has no partner, reject al-Lat and al-ʿUzza, and free yourself of false gods.” ʿAli did so, and accepted Islam.',
  'Ibn Ishaq. Al-Lat and al-ʿUzza were idols of Makkah; the game names them only as the text does and never shows them.'),
 ('f-zayd', 6, 'وأسلم ابن حارثة - يعني زيدا -', 'يختلف علي إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم', 'established',
  'Ibn Haritha, meaning Zayd, accepted Islam too. The two of them stayed like that for nearly a month, with ʿAli coming and going to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.',
  ''),
 ('f-afif', 6, 'كنت امرءا تاجرا فقدمت منى أيام الحج', 'فكنت أكون ثانيا .', 'established',
  '[ʿAfif said:] I was a merchant, and I came to Mina in the days of the Hajj. Al-ʿAbbas ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib was a merchant too, and I went to him to buy and sell. While we were with him, a man came out of a tent and stood praying facing the Kaʿbah. Then a woman came out and stood praying with him, and a boy came out and stood praying with him. I said: “ʿAbbas, what is this religion? We do not know what it is.” He said: “This is Muhammad ibn ʿAbdullah. He says that Allah has sent him with it, and that the treasures of Kisra and Qaysar will be opened to him. This is his wife Khadija bint Khuwaylid, who believes in him, and this boy is his cousin ʿAli ibn Abi Talib, who believes in him.” ʿAfif said: “If only I had believed that day, so that I could have been the second.”',
  'Ibn Ishaq, through Yunus ibn Bukayr. Nothing of this scene is shown in the game.'),
 ('f-first4', 6, 'فكان أول من بادر إلى التصديق من الرجال الأحرار أبو بكر الصديق', 'رضي الله عنهم وأرضاهم .', 'established',
  'The first to hurry to believe were: of the free men, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq; of the boys, ʿAli ibn Abi Talib; of the women, Khadija bint Khuwaylid, his wife, peace be upon him; and of the freed slaves, his freed slave Zayd ibn Haritha al-Kalbi, may Allah be pleased with them all.',
  'Ibn Kathir’s own words.'),
 ('f-others', 6, 'وقال آخرون: أول من أسلم من هذه الأمة أبو بكر الصديق', 'أبو بكر الصديق', 'reported',
  'Others said: the first of this Ummah to accept Islam was Abu Bakr al-Siddiq.',
  'One of the views Ibn Kathir lists before bringing them together.'),
 ('f-kalbi', 6, 'قال الكلبي: أسلم وهو ابن تسع سنين.', 'ابن تسع سنين.', 'reported',
  'Al-Kalbi said: he [ʿAli] accepted Islam when he was nine years old.',
  'Other reports say ten; the book gives more than one age.'),
 ('f-abubakr', 6, 'وأول من أسلم من الرجال الأحرار أبو بكر الصديق، وإسلامه كان أنفع', 'يبذل المال في طاعة الله ورسوله', 'established',
  'The first of the free men to accept Islam was Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and his Islam brought more benefit than that of those mentioned before, because he was a man of high standing, an honoured chief in Quraysh, a man of wealth, and one who called others to Islam. He was loved and brought people together, and spent his wealth in obedience to Allah and His Messenger.',
  'Ibn Kathir’s own words.'),
 ('f-trader', 6, 'وكان رجلا تاجرا ذا خلق ومعروف', 'وحسن مجالسته.', 'established',
  '[Abu Bakr] was a merchant of good character and kindness. The men of his people would come to him and enjoy his company for more than one reason: his knowledge, his trade and his good company.',
  'Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('f-five', 6, 'فجعل يدعو إلى الإسلام من وثق به من قومه', 'وعبد الرحمن بن عوف رضي الله عنهم', 'reported',
  'He began to call to Islam those of his people he trusted, who came and sat with him. At his hands, as I have been told, these accepted Islam: al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwam, ʿUthman ibn ʿAffan, Talha ibn ʿUbaydullah, Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas and ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with them.',
  'Ibn Ishaq gives this with “fima balaghani”, “as I have been told”, so it is shown as a report.'),
 ('f-eight', 6, 'فانطلقوا إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ومعهم أبو بكر.', 'وآمنوا بما جاء من عند الله.', 'established',
  'They went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with Abu Bakr. He offered them Islam, recited the Qurʾan to them and told them the truth of Islam, and they believed. These were the eight who came before [the people] to Islam. They believed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and believed in what came from Allah.',
  ''),
 ('f-spread', 6, 'ثم دخل الناس [في الإسلام] أرسالا', 'بمكة وتحدث به.', 'established',
  'Then people entered [Islam] in group after group, men and women, until the news of Islam spread in Makkah and people talked about it.',
  ''),
 ('f-shiab', 6, 'قال وكان أصحاب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا صلوا ذهبوا في الشعاب', 'بصلاتهم من قومهم.', 'established',
  'When the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, they would go out into the mountain passes and hide their prayer from their people.',
  ''),
 ('f-three', 6, 'قال ابن إسحاق: ثم أمر الله رسوله صلى الله عليه وسلم بعد ثلاث سنين من البعثة', 'على أذى المشركين.', 'established',
  'Ibn Ishaq said: Then, three years after he was sent, Allah commanded His Messenger ﷺ to proclaim openly what he had been commanded, and to be patient with the harm of the idol-worshippers.',
  ''),
 ('f-safa', 6, 'لما أنزل الله: (وأنذر عشيرتك الأقربين) أتى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم الصفا', 'بين يدي عذاب شديد "', 'established',
  '[Ibn ʿAbbas said:] When Allah sent down “And warn your nearest relatives” [al-Shuʿaraʾ 214], the Prophet ﷺ came to al-Safa, climbed it, and called out: “Ya sabahah!”, the call of alarm. The people gathered to him, some coming themselves and some sending someone. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Banu ʿAbd al-Muttalib, Banu Fihr, Banu Kaʿb, if I told you that horsemen at the foot of this mountain wanted to attack you, would you believe me?” They said: “Yes.” He said: “Then I am a warner to you, before a severe punishment.”',
  'From Imam Ahmad; also in al-Bukhari and Muslim. The words of the verse are given in meaning.'),

 # ── Episode 7 · The Courage of Bilal (RA) ──
 ('bl-hard', 7, 'قال ابن إسحاق: ثم إنهم عدوا على من أسلم', 'ويعصمه الله منهم', 'established',
  'Ibn Ishaq said: Then they turned on those of his Companions who had accepted Islam and followed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Every clan set upon the Muslims among them, imprisoning them and tormenting them with beatings, hunger and thirst, and with the burning ground of Makkah when the heat was fierce: those they could overpower, to turn them away from their religion. Some gave way because of the severity of what struck them, and some stood firm against them, and Allah protected them.',
  'Told in words only: nothing of it is shown or played.'),
 ('bl-who', 7, 'فكان بلال مولى أبي بكر لبعض بني جمح', 'وكان صادق الإسلام طاهر القلب', 'established',
  'Bilal, [later] the freed slave of Abu Bakr, belonged to some of Banu Jumah, born a slave among them. He was Bilal ibn Rabah, and his mother’s name was Hamama. He was true in his Islam and pure of heart.',
  'Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('bl-seven', 7, 'قال أول من أظهر الإسلام سبعة', 'وهو يقول: أحد أحد.', 'established',
  '[Ibn Masʿud] said: The first to show their Islam openly were seven: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, ʿAmmar, his mother Sumayya, Suhayb, Bilal and al-Miqdad. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Allah protected him through his uncle [Abu Talib], and Abu Bakr, Allah protected him through his people. As for the rest, the idol-worshippers seized them, put iron armour on them and left them in the scorching sun. Every one of them gave them what they wanted [with their tongues], except Bilal: his own self meant little to him for the sake of Allah, and he meant little to his people. They took him and handed him to the boys, who led him around the passes of Makkah while he said: “Ahad, Ahad”, One, One.',
  'Narrated by Imam Ahmad and Ibn Majah. Told in words only: nothing of it is shown or played.'),
 ('bl-waraqa', 7, 'كان ورقة بن نوفل يمر به وهو يعذب لذلك', 'لأتخذنه حنانا .', 'reported',
  'Waraqa ibn Nawfal used to pass by him while he was being tormented for it and was saying “Ahad, Ahad”, and Waraqa would say: “One, One, by Allah, Bilal.” Then he would turn to Umayya ibn Khalaf and those of Banu Jumah who did that to him and say: “I swear by Allah, if you kill him for this, I will make his grave a place of mercy.”',
  'Ibn Kathir questions this report, as the next passage shows.'),
 ('bl-doubt', 7, 'قلت: قد استشكل بعضهم هذا', 'وفيه نظر .', 'established',
  'I say: Some have found this difficult, because Waraqa died after the sending, during the pause in revelation, and people only accepted Islam after “O you wrapped up” was sent down. So how could Waraqa have passed by Bilal while he was tormented? This needs looking into.',
  'Ibn Kathir’s own words.'),
 ('bl-freed', 7, 'ثم ذكر ابن إسحاق مرور أبي بكر ببلال وهو يعذب', 'فأعتقه وأراحه من العذاب', 'established',
  'Then Ibn Ishaq mentions Abu Bakr passing by Bilal while he was being tormented. He bought him from Umayya in exchange for a slave of his, set him free, and released him from the torment.',
  ''),
 ('bl-nahdiyya', 7, 'والنهدية وابنتها اشتراها من بني عبد الدار', '[أو] ذلك إن شئتما.', 'established',
  'And al-Nahdiyya and her daughter: he bought them from Banu ʿAbd al-Dar. Their mistress had sent them to grind flour for her, and he heard her say to them: “By Allah, I will never free you.” Abu Bakr said: “Release yourself from that oath, Umm so-and-so.” She said: “You release yourself! You have spoilt them, so free them yourself.” He said: “How much are they?” She said: “Such and such.” He said: “I have taken them, and they are free. Give her back her flour.” They said: “Shall we not finish grinding it first, Abu Bakr, and then give it back to her?” He said: “That too, if you wish.”',
  'Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('bl-quhafa', 7, 'قال قال أبو قحافة لابنه أبي بكر', 'يا أبة إني إنما أريد ما أريد.', 'established',
  'Abu Quhafa said to his son Abu Bakr: “My son, I see you freeing weak people. If only, when you did what you did, you had freed strong men who would protect you and stand up for you.” Abu Bakr said: “Father, I only want what I want.”',
  'Ibn Ishaq, from the family of ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr.'),
 ('bl-verses', 7, 'فتحدث أنه ما أنزل هؤلاء الآيات إلا فيه وفيما قال أبوه', 'وفيما قال أبوه', 'reported',
  'It is said that these verses were sent down about him and what his father said: [Surah al-Layl, verses 5 to 7].',
  '“It is said”, so it is shown as a report. The verses are recited by the reciter in the game.'),
 ('bl-yasir', 7, 'وكانت بنو مخزوم يخرجون بعمار بن ياسر', 'موعدكم الجنة "', 'reported',
  'Banu Makhzum used to take ʿAmmar ibn Yasir out, with his father and his mother, who were a family of Islam, when the midday heat was fierce, and torment them on the burning ground of Makkah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pass by them and say, as I have been told: “Patience, family of Yasir: your meeting place is Paradise.”',
  'Ibn Ishaq gives the words with “fima balaghani”, “as I have been told”, so it is shown as a report.'),

 # ── Episode 8 · Escape to Abyssinia ──
 ('ab-why', 8, 'فلما رأى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ما يصيب أصحابه من البلاء', 'وفرارا إلى الله بدينهم.', 'established',
  'When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw the trials that struck his Companions, while he himself was kept safe by his standing with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and by his uncle Abu Talib, and that he could not protect them from what they were suffering, he said to them: “What if you went out to the land of Abyssinia? There is a king there under whom no one is wronged, and it is a land of truthfulness, until Allah gives you a way out of what you are in.” So the Muslims among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the land of Abyssinia, fearing to be turned from their religion, and fleeing to Allah with their religion.',
  'Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('ab-first', 8, 'فكانت أول هجرة كانت في الإسلام', 'وزوجته رقية بنت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم .', 'established',
  'It was the first emigration in Islam. The first of the Muslims to go out were ʿUthman ibn ʿAffan and his wife Ruqayya, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.',
  ''),
 ('ab-when', 8, 'وروى الواقدي أن خروجهم إليها في رجب سنة خمس من البعثة', 'فاستأجروا سفينة بنصف دينار إلى الحبشة.', 'established',
  'Al-Waqidi narrated that they set out for it in Rajab, in the fifth year after the sending, and that the first of them to emigrate were eleven men and four women. They reached the sea, some walking and some riding, and hired a ship to Abyssinia for half a dinar.',
  ''),
 ('ab-82', 8, 'وقال آخرون بل كانوا اثنين وثمانين رجلا', 'ثلاثة وثمانين رجلا.', 'reported',
  'Others said: rather, they were eighty-two men, besides their wives and children, and ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, about whom there is doubt. If he was among them, they were eighty-three men.',
  'Another view, given by Ibn Jarir.'),
 ('ab-umm', 8, 'فخرجنا إليها أرسالا حتى اجتمعنا بها', 'ولم نخش فيها ظلما.', 'established',
  '[Umm Salama said:] So we went out to it in groups until we had all gathered there. We settled in the best of homes, next to the best of neighbours, safe in our religion, and we feared no wrong there.',
  'From the account of Umm Salama, through Ibn Ishaq.'),
 ('ab-envoys', 8, 'فلما رأت قريش أنا قد أصبنا دارا وأمنا', 'إلا هيئوا له هدية على حدة', 'established',
  'When Quraysh saw that we had found a home and safety, they were jealous of us. They agreed to send to the Negus about us, to have us driven out of his land and sent back to them. They sent ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs and ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Rabiʿa, and gathered gifts for him and for his generals, and did not leave out a single general without preparing a gift for him.',
  ''),
 ('ab-leather', 8, 'وكان من أحب ما يهدون إليه من مكة الأدم', 'الأدم', 'established',
  'One of the gifts from Makkah he liked best was leather.',
  ''),
 ('ab-fair', 8, 'فغضب ثم قال: لا لعمر الله! لا أردهم عليهم حتى أدعوهم', 'ولم أنعم عينا', 'established',
  'He became angry and said: “No, by the life of Allah! I will not hand them back until I have called them, spoken to them and seen what their matter is. They are people who sought refuge in my land and chose my protection over anyone else’s. If they are as these men say, I will hand them back. If they are otherwise, I will protect them and not come between them and their people, and I will not please these two.”',
  ''),
 ('ab-ask', 8, 'فقال له النجاشي: ما هذا الدين الذي أنتم عليه؟', 'ولا نعبد غيره.', 'established',
  'The Negus said to him: “What is this religion you follow? You have left the religion of your people, and you have not entered Judaism or Christianity.” Jaʿfar said: “O King, we were a people of idol-worship. We worshipped idols, ate dead animals and treated our neighbours badly. We made lawful among ourselves what is sacred, shedding blood and more, holding nothing lawful or unlawful. Then Allah sent us a prophet from among ourselves, whose faithfulness, truthfulness and trustworthiness we knew. He called us to worship Allah alone, with no partner, to keep the ties of kinship, to protect our neighbours, and to pray and fast for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and to worship none but Him.”',
  ''),
 ('ab-taught', 8, 'وقال زياد عن ابن إسحاق: فدعانا إلى الله لنوحده', 'وأمرنا بالصلاة والزكاة والصيام.', 'established',
  'In Ziyad’s version from Ibn Ishaq: “He called us to Allah, to declare Him One and worship Him, and to leave the stones and idols that we and our fathers worshipped besides Him. He commanded us to speak the truth, to fulfil trusts, to keep the ties of kinship, to be good neighbours, and to keep away from forbidden things and from bloodshed. He forbade us shameful deeds, false speech, eating the wealth of the orphan and slandering chaste women. He commanded us to worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and commanded us prayer, zakat and fasting.”',
  ''),
 ('ab-chose', 8, 'فصدقناه وآمنا به واتبعناه', 'ورجونا أن لا نظلم عندك أيها الملك.', 'established',
  '“So we believed him, had faith in him and followed him in what he brought from Allah. We worshipped Allah alone with no partner and associated nothing with Him, held unlawful what was made unlawful for us and lawful what was made lawful for us. Then our people attacked us and tormented us, to turn us from our religion, to take us back from the worship of Allah to the worship of idols, and to make lawful again the bad things we used to make lawful. When they overpowered us, wronged us, made life narrow for us and came between us and our religion, we came out to your land. We chose you over everyone else, wished for your protection, and hoped we would not be wronged with you, O King.”',
  ''),
 ('ab-maryam', 8, 'قال هلم فاتل علي مما جاء به', 'ولا أنعمكم عينا .', 'established',
  'He said: “Come, recite to me some of what he brought.” So he recited to him the beginning of Kaf Ha Ya ʿAyn Sad [Surah Maryam]. By Allah, the Negus wept until his beard was wet, and his bishops wept until their scrolls were wet [when they heard what he recited to them]. Then he said [to them]: “This speech comes from the same lamp-niche that Musa brought. Go, rightly guided. No, by Allah, I will not hand them over to you, and I will not please you.”',
  'The book does not say how many verses were recited; the game plays the opening verses of Surah Maryam from the reciter.'),
 ('ab-isa', 8, 'فقال له جعفر: نقول هو عبد الله ورسوله وروحه وكلمته', 'مما قلت هذا العويد.', 'established',
  'Jaʿfar said to him: “We say: he is the servant of Allah and His messenger, His spirit and His word, which He cast into Maryam, the virgin, devoted to Allah.” The Negus lowered his hand to the ground and took a little stick between his fingers, and said: “ʿIsa ibn Maryam is not more than what you have said by the length of this little stick.”',
  ''),
 ('ab-safe', 8, 'اذهبوا فأنتم سيوم في الأرض', 'وأني آذيت رجلا منكم', 'established',
  '“Go: you are suyum in the land”, that is, safe in the land. “Whoever insults you will pay a fine. Whoever insults you will pay a fine. Whoever insults you will pay a fine”, three times. “I would not want a dabr, and to have hurt one man of you.”',
  'The book explains that dabr means gold in their language; another version says “a mountain of gold”.'),

 # ── Episode 9 · The Years of Hardship ──
 ('h-shib', 9, 'فلما رأى أبو طالب عمل القوم جمع بني عبد المطلب', 'ومنهم من فعله إيمانا ويقينا.', 'established',
  'When Abu Talib saw what the people were doing, he gathered Banu ʿAbd al-Muttalib and told them to bring the Messenger of Allah ﷺ into their valley and protect him from those who wanted to kill him. The Muslims and the non-Muslims among them agreed on this: some did it out of family loyalty, and some out of faith and certainty.',
  'Musa ibn ʿUqba, from al-Zuhri.'),
 ('h-pact', 9, 'اجتمع المشركون من قريش فأجمعوا أمرهم أن لا يجالسوهم', 'حتى يسلموه للقتل.', 'established',
  'The idol-worshippers of Quraysh met and agreed not to sit with them, not to trade with them and not to enter their houses, until they handed over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to be killed. In their plotting they wrote a document of pledges and promises never to accept peace from Banu Hashim and never to show them pity until they handed him over.',
  ''),
 ('h-years', 9, 'فلبث بنو هاشم في شعبهم ثلاث سنين', 'إلا بادروهم إليه فاشتروه', 'established',
  'Banu Hashim stayed in their valley for three years, and the hardship and strain on them grew severe. Quraysh cut them off from the markets: of the food that came to Makkah and the goods for sale, they let none reach them, but hurried to it first and bought it.',
  'Told in words only: no hunger or suffering is shown or played.'),
 ('h-bed', 9, 'فكان أبو طالب إذا أخذ الناس مضاجعهم', 'فينام عليه', 'established',
  'When people went to bed, Abu Talib would tell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to lie down on his own bed, so that anyone who meant to harm him by a plot or a sudden attack would see it. Then, when people were asleep, he would tell one of his sons, his brothers or his cousins to lie down on the bed of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and tell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to go to one of their beds and sleep there.',
  ''),
 ('h-regret', 9, 'فلما كان رأس ثلاث سنين تلاوم رجال من بني عبد مناف', 'من الغدر والبراءة منه', 'established',
  'At the end of three years, men of Banu ʿAbd Manaf, of Qusayy, and others of Quraysh whose mothers were from Banu Hashim, began to blame one another. They saw that they had cut the ties of kinship and made light of what was right. That same night they agreed to break the treacherous pact they had made, and to free themselves of it.',
  ''),
 ('h-termite', 9, 'وبعث الله على صحيفتهم الأرضة', 'من عهد وميثاق.', 'established',
  'And Allah sent the termite against their document, and it ate away every pledge and promise in it.',
  'An act of Allah: the game shows nothing of it.'),
